NNAAMMEE
     ccaarrpp — Common Address Redundancy Protocol

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     ddeevviiccee ccaarrpp

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The CARP allows multiple hosts on the same local network to share a set
     of IPv4 and/or IPv6 addresses.  Its primary purpose is to ensure that
     these addresses are always available.

     To use ccaarrpp, the administrator needs to configure at a minimum a common
     virtual host ID (vhid), and attach at least one IP address to this vhid
     on each machine which is to take part in the virtual group.  Additional
     parameters can also be set on a per-vhid basis: aaddvvbbaassee and aaddvvsskkeeww,
     which are used to control how frequently the host sends advertisements
     when it is the master for a virtual host, and ppaassss which is used to au‐
     thenticate ccaarrpp advertisements.  The aaddvvbbaassee parameter stands for
     “advertisement base”.  It is measured in seconds and specifies the base
     of the advertisement interval.  The aaddvvsskkeeww parameter stands for
     “advertisement skew”.  It is measured in 1/256 of seconds.  It is added
     to the base advertisement interval to make one host advertise a bit
     slower that the other does.  Both aaddvvbbaassee and aaddvvsskkeeww are put inside CARP
     advertisements.  These values can be configured using ifconfig(8), or
     through the SIOCSVH ioctl(2).

     CARP defaults to using multicast messages, but can be configured to uni‐
     cast announcements to peers using the ppeeeerr and ppeeeerr66 parameters. Default
     addresses can be restored using mmccaasstt and mmccaasstt66.  Note that TTL verifi‐
     cation is disabled if the peer address is not a multicast address.  These
     values can be configured using ifconfig(8), or through the SIOCSPEER
     ioctl(2).

     CARP virtual hosts can be configured on multicast-capable interfaces:
     Ethernet, layer 2 VLAN, FDDI and Token Ring.  An arbitrary number of vir‐
     tual host IDs can be configured on an interface.  An arbitrary number of
     IPv4 or IPv6 addresses can be attached to a particular vhid.  It is im‐
     portant that all hosts participating in a vhid have the same list of pre‐
     fixes configured on the vhid, since all the prefixes are included in the
     cryptographic checksum supplied in each advertisement.  Multiple vhids
     running on one interface participate in master/backup elections indepen‐
     dently.

     Additionally, there are a number of global parameters which can be set
     using sysctl(8):

